Hash keys and load metadata.

Every table in the vault — hub, link, and satellite — carries the same set of metadata columns. And they're not decoration: they're the machinery that delivers the vault's two promises — parallel loading, and auditability.

Start with the hash key. The surrogate primary key of every hub and link is a hash key — an M-D-five or S-H-A hash of the business key, or keys. So the customer hash key is a hash of the customer i-d. The order-line hash key is a hash of the order hash key plus the product hash key. Why hash, instead of a database sequence? Because a hash is deterministic: the same business key produces the same hash on any system, with no lookup. And that single property is what lets hubs, links, and satellites load independently and in parallel. A link can compute its parent hash keys itself, without first querying the hubs to find a sequence number. It's what makes the vault scale on distributed, massively-parallel platforms.

Then there's the load metadata, on every row. Load date — when the row entered the vault; and sequencing satellite rows by load date is the history. And record source — where the row came from: web, POS, ERP. Every single row carries its origin, so lineage is never lost. Together, load date plus record source on every row is the audit trail: for any value, you can say exactly when it arrived, and from which system.

Satellites add one more column: hash-diff — a hash of all the descriptive attributes in the row. On load, you compare the incoming hash-diff to the parent's latest satellite row. If it differs, something changed — so you insert a new satellite version. If it matches, nothing changed — so you skip. It's the same hash trick from the SCD-2 merge back in module six, and it means you never diff columns one by one. So: the hash key makes loads lookup-free and parallel; load date and record source stamp every row for audit; and hash-diff detects change in a single comparison. This metadata is how the vault delivers both scale and auditability.
